    "content": "Palm Sunday commemorates Jesus's triumphal entry into Jerusalem, marking the start of Holy Week. Riding a humble donkey to fulfill prophecy, Jesus was welcomed by crowds laying palm branches and cloaks on the road, shouting \"Hosanna\" and hailing him as King. It marks his final week before his crucifixion and resurrection. Key Aspects of the Palm Sunday Story: The Triumphal Entry: Jesus approached Jerusalem from the Mount of Olives and sent disciples to find a donkey colt, fulfilling the prophecy of Zechariah 9:9 that the King would come lowly and riding on a donkey, symbolizing peace rather than war. Welcoming Crowd: A large crowd, having heard of his miracles (especially raising Lazarus), gathered to welcome him, spreading palm branches—symbols of victory, peace, and triumph—and their own cloaks on the road to form a carpet. \"Hosanna\" Praise: The people shouted, \"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord!\". \"Hosanna\" is a Hebrew phrase meaning \"save us\" or \"save now\". Symbolism of Humility: By riding a donkey instead of a horse or chariot, Jesus demonstrated that he was a humble king and that his kingdom was not of this world. Mixed Reactions: While the crowds cheered, religious leaders (Pharisees) questioned the praise, but Jesus told them that if the people kept quiet, the rocks would cry out. The Aftermath: After entering the city, Jesus went to the Temple, where he drove out the money changers who were exploiting worshippers. Significance: Palm Sunday bridges the joy of Christ's arrival with the coming sorrow of his passion and death (Good Friday). Palm Sunday is celebrated on the Sunday before Easter, with churches often distributing palm fronds during the service.",
